###############################################################################
# AI-Library API — Chat HTTP tests
# Tool: Visual Studio / VS Code REST Client / Rider HTTP Client
#
# Prerequisites:
#   1. Ollama running locally with model pulled (default: gemma3:4b)
#   2. API started:  dotnet run --project src/AiLibrary.Api --launch-profile http
#   3. Base URL below matches launchSettings (http profile = port 5243)
#
# How multi-turn works:
#   - First request may omit sessionId (server creates one).
#   - Copy sessionId from the response into later requests so history is reused.
#   - History is in-memory on the API process (lost on restart).
###############################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5243
@contentType = application/json

### ============================================================================
### Scenario A — Health / OpenAPI smoke test
### Why: Confirm the API process is up before spending time on model calls.
### Expected: 200 + OpenAPI JSON document.
### ============================================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/openapi/v1.json
Accept: application/json

### ============================================================================
### Scenario B — Start a new conversation (no sessionId)
### Why: First turn; server should allocate a sessionId and seed the librarian
###       system prompt into history. Ava should introduce / answer as librarian.
### Expected: 200 JSON { "sessionId": "...", "message": "..." }
### Action: Copy sessionId from response into @sessionId below for multi-turn.
### ============================================================================
# @name startChat
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/chat
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "message": "Hello! Tell me briefly about the book The Hobbit by J.R.R. Tolkien without major spoilers."
}

###
# After running Scenario B, set this from the response body (REST Client:
# @sessionId = {{startChat.response.body.sessionId}} )
@sessionId = {{startChat.response.body.$.sessionId}}

### ============================================================================
### Scenario C — Multi-turn #2: ask for similar books (SAME session)
### Why: Proves conversation history works. The model should remember The Hobbit
###       from Scenario B without repeating the full title context.
### Expected: 200, same sessionId, recommendations related to The Hobbit.
### ============================================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/chat
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "sessionId": "{{sessionId}}",
  "message": "What are three similar books I might enjoy if I liked that one?"
}

### ============================================================================
### Scenario D — Multi-turn #3: ask for a summary of a prior recommendation
### Why: Harder memory check — refers to "the first similar book you recommended"
###       which only exists in session history, not in this request body.
### Expected: 200, same sessionId, summary of one of the three titles from C.
### ============================================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/chat
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "sessionId": "{{sessionId}}",
  "message": "Give me a short spoiler-free summary of the first similar book you recommended."
}

### ============================================================================
### Scenario E — New session isolation
### Why: Omitting sessionId must NOT reuse Scenario B–D history.
### Expected: New sessionId; assistant should NOT assume you already discussed Hobbit
###           (may still know the book generally, but not "that one" from prior chat).
### ============================================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/chat
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "message": "What book were we just talking about?"
}

### ============================================================================
### Scenario F — Validation: empty message
### Why: API should reject empty input instead of calling the model.
### Expected: 400 Bad Request (model binding / [Required] validation).
### ============================================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/chat
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "message": ""
}

### ============================================================================
### Scenario G — Streaming (SSE) first turn
### Why: Angular "Stream replies" toggle uses POST /api/chat/stream.
###       Events: session → token* → done
### Expected: Content-Type text/event-stream; event lines in body.
### Note: Some HTTP clients buffer SSE; prefer browser Network tab or curl -N.
### ============================================================================
# @name streamStart
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/chat/stream
Content-Type: {{contentType}}
Accept: text/event-stream

{
  "message": "Name one classic mystery novel in one short sentence."
}

###
@streamSessionId = {{streamStart.response.body.$.sessionId}}

### ============================================================================
### Scenario H — Streaming multi-turn (same session as G if client captured id)
### Why: Streaming path must share the same IChatHistoryStore as JSON chat.
### Expected: Tokens stream; history includes previous stream turn.
### Manual: paste sessionId from the "session" SSE event of Scenario G.
### ============================================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/chat/stream
Content-Type: {{contentType}}
Accept: text/event-stream

{
  "sessionId": "PASTE_STREAM_SESSION_ID_HERE",
  "message": "Recommend one similar book to that mystery novel."
}

### ============================================================================
### Scenario I — CORS preflight (Angular dev origin)
### Why: Browser from http://localhost:4200 sends OPTIONS before POST.
### Expected: 204/200 with Access-Control-Allow-Origin including localhost:4200.
### ============================================================================
OPTIONS {{baseUrl}}/api/chat
Origin: http://localhost:4200
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type
